Your friend sounds a little like an ass but I understand her situation, I am half Vietnamese myself and I struggle with my identity a lot, not feeling accept by white people or Asian people. I really do *completely* sympathize with her situation. However, the way she treats her fellow Asian piers is completely inappropriate despite it being very clear to me that she does have deep insecurities about her race. I agree with you OP that she probably should *not* be applying for that scholarship. You certainly are not racist by expressing that. She maybe right that you have no place to tell her that though. It's possibly unethical for her to apply for it but it's indisputable that despite being raised by white parents I assume to be fairly well off (international adoptions are very pricey) she has experienced some forms of racism and prejudice that have damaged her, and likely hindered her in one way or another. She could feel you are telling her because she has privileged parents she has had it easy. I'm sure that was not your intention.

&amp;#x200B;

Those experiences in my opinion still likely does not make her the type of person the scholarship is likely meant for but what can you do? I recommend not interfering anymore. She is right in a sense that you don't have a right to tell her what to do. But you are most certainly not the asshole. If anyone is it is your dear friend. 

